::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A final score uses tests at 70% and homework at 30%. Scores are 90 and 80. What is the weighted average?
{
=A. 87#Correct. 90(0.70) + 80(0.30) = 63 + 24 = 87.
~B. 85#This is the simple average, not the weighted average.
~C. 170#This adds the scores without applying weights.
~D. 60#This does not apply the weights correctly.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Which decimal weight is equivalent to 45%?
{
~A. 4.5#This represents 450%, not 45%.
=B. 0.45#Correct. 45% equals 45 divided by 100, or 0.45.
~C. 0.045#This represents 4.5%, not 45%.
~D. 45.0#This is not a valid decimal weight for 45%.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A score uses 60% for performance and 40% for interview. The scores are 75 and 90. What is the weighted score?
{
~A. 82.5#This is the simple average, but performance and interview have different weights.
~B. 165#This adds the two scores without weights.
=C. 81#Correct. 75(0.60) + 90(0.40) = 45 + 36 = 81.
~D. 54#This omits part of the weighted calculation.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
What does a 60% weight mean in a decision score?
{
~A. The category is ignored#A 60% weight means the category matters strongly.
~B. The category counts as 6%#60% is 0.60 of the decision, not 0.06.
~C. The score must be 60#The weight is not the score; it is how much the category counts.
=D. The category counts for 60 out of 100 parts#Correct. A 60% weight means that category contributes 60% of the total.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Two weighted scores are 91 and 88. Which is stronger if higher scores are better?
{
=A. 91#Correct. A higher weighted score indicates the stronger option.
~B. 88#This is lower, so it is not stronger when higher scores are better.
~C. Both are the same#The scores are different.
~D. Neither can be compared#Weighted scores can be compared when they use the same criteria.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A grade uses participation at 20%, quizzes at 30%, and projects at 50%. Scores are 100, 80, and 90. What is the weighted average?
{
~A. 90#This may be a simple estimate, but the weighted calculation gives a different result.
=B. 89#Correct. 100(0.20) + 80(0.30) + 90(0.50) = 20 + 24 + 45 = 89.
~C. 270#This adds all scores without weights.
~D. 54#This omits major parts of the weighted calculation.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Why is a simple average not always appropriate?
{
~A. It is always incorrect#A simple average is useful when categories have equal importance.
~B. It ignores the original scores#A simple average uses scores, but treats them equally.
=C. It treats all categories as equally important#Correct. Weighted averages are needed when categories have different importance.
~D. It always creates a lower result#A simple average may be higher or lower depending on the values.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A weighted score uses cost 50%, quality 30%, and speed 20%. Scores are 80, 90, and 70. What is the weighted score?
{
~A. 80#This is close to the simple average but does not apply all weights correctly.
~B. 240#This adds the scores without weights.
~C. 86#This overweights quality and does not match the given weights.
=D. 81#Correct. 80(0.50) + 90(0.30) + 70(0.20) = 40 + 27 + 14 = 81.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Which set of weights totals 100%?
{
=A. 50%, 30%, 20%#Correct. 50 + 30 + 20 = 100%.
~B. 60%, 30%, 20%#These weights total 110%, which is too much.
~C. 40%, 20%, 20%#These weights total 80%, so part of the decision is missing.
~D. 25%, 25%, 25%#These weights total 75%, not 100%.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A candidate has scores 92 at 40% and 80 at 60%. What is the weighted score?
{
~A. 86#This is the simple average and does not apply weights.
=B. 84.8#Correct. 92(0.40) + 80(0.60) = 36.8 + 48 = 84.8.
~C. 172#This adds the scores without applying weights.
~D. 48#This uses only the second weighted product.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
What spreadsheet setup best supports a weighted-average calculation?
{
~A. Scores only#Scores alone do not show how much each category counts.
~B. Weights only#Weights alone do not include the category scores.
=C. Scores, weights, and score times weight#Correct. Weighted averages require each score, each weight, and each weighted product.
~D. Category names only#Names alone do not allow calculation.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A score uses 25%, 25%, and 50% weights. Scores are 60, 80, and 90. What is the weighted average?
{
~A. 76.7#This is the simple average, not the weighted average.
~B. 230#This adds the scores without applying weights.
~C. 57.5#This omits part of the weighted sum.
=D. 80#Correct. 60(0.25) + 80(0.25) + 90(0.50) = 15 + 20 + 45 = 80.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Why might a category with a lower score still strongly affect the weighted average?
{
=A. It may have a large weight#Correct. A low score in a heavily weighted category can strongly affect the result.
~B. Lower scores are always ignored#Lower scores still count according to their weights.
~C. Weights only apply to high scores#Weights apply to every category score.
~D. Weighted averages do not use category scores#Weighted averages use both scores and weights.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A job option has pay 90 at 60% and commute 70 at 40%. What is the weighted score?
{
~A. 80#This is the simple average, not the weighted average.
=B. 82#Correct. 90(0.60) + 70(0.40) = 54 + 28 = 82.
~C. 160#This adds the scores without weights.
~D. 74#This reverses or misapplies the weights.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Which calculation matches scores 88 and 76 with weights 75% and 25%?
{
~A. 88 + 76#This adds the scores without applying weights.
~B. 88(0.25) + 76(0.75)#This reverses the weights.
=C. 88(0.75) + 76(0.25)#Correct. Each score is multiplied by its matching weight.
~D. 88(75) + 76(25)#This uses percent numbers instead of decimal weights.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A weighted result is 73.5. Which interpretation is best?
{
~A. The result must be rounded to 100#There is no reason to round the score to 100.
~B. The result is one category score only#A weighted result combines categories.
~C. The result ignores weights#A weighted result is based on weights.
=D. The overall weighted score is 73.5 points#Correct. The weighted result summarizes the category scores using their weights.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Scores are 100 at 10% and 70 at 90%. What is the weighted average?
{
=A. 73#Correct. 100(0.10) + 70(0.90) = 10 + 63 = 73.
~B. 85#This is the simple average, not the weighted average.
~C. 170#This adds the scores without weights.
~D. 100#This uses only the smaller weighted category.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
What is the first step in calculating a weighted average?
{
~A. Add all scores with no weights#This would produce a total, not a weighted average.
=B. Convert percent weights to decimals#Correct. Decimal weights are needed before multiplying scores by weights.
~C. Choose the largest score#Weighted averages combine all categories.
~D. Ignore categories with small weights#Every category should be included according to its weight.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A student has 95 in homework weighted 20% and 75 in tests weighted 80%. What is the weighted average?
{
~A. 85#This is the simple average, not the weighted average.
~B. 170#This adds the two scores.
=C. 79#Correct. 95(0.20) + 75(0.80) = 19 + 60 = 79.
~D. 95#This uses only the homework score and ignores the larger test weight.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Which statement supports using technology or a spreadsheet?
{
~A. It removes the need to understand weights#Students still need to understand the meaning of weights.
~B. It should hide all calculations#A useful spreadsheet shows or organizes calculations.
~C. It changes incorrect weights into correct ones automatically#Technology does not replace checking setup and meaning.
=D. It helps organize scores, weights, and weighted products#Correct. Spreadsheets support accurate organization and calculation.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
An option has score 84 at 50%, 90 at 25%, and 70 at 25%. What is the weighted score?
{
=A. 82#Correct. 84(0.50) + 90(0.25) + 70(0.25) = 42 + 22.5 + 17.5 = 82.
~B. 81.3#This is the simple average, not the weighted score.
~C. 244#This adds all scores without weights.
~D. 61#This omits part of the weighted calculation.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Which category affects the total most if weights are 15%, 25%, and 60%?
{
~A. The 15% category#This is the smallest weight.
=B. The 60% category#Correct. The largest weight has the strongest effect on the weighted average.
~C. The 25% category#This matters, but less than the 60% category.
~D. All categories affect it equally#Different weights mean categories do not affect the total equally.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
If weights total 120%, what is the issue?
{
~A. The categories do not matter#The categories may matter, but the weights are not properly scaled.
~B. The scores must all be zero#Incorrect weights do not force scores to zero.
=C. The weights account for more than the whole decision#Correct. Weights should total 100%, not 120%.
~D. The weighted average must be 120#The total weight does not directly become the score.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A course uses quizzes 30%, assignments 30%, and final exam 40%. Scores are 80, 90, and 70. What is the weighted average?
{
~A. 80#This is the simple average and does not reflect the 40% exam weight.
~B. 240#This adds the scores without applying weights.
~C. 72#This omits part of the calculation.
=D. 79#Correct. 80(0.30) + 90(0.30) + 70(0.40) = 24 + 27 + 28 = 79.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Why is a weighted score useful for comparing options?
{
=A. It combines several criteria while honoring their importance#Correct. Weighted scores account for both performance and category importance.
~B. It always chooses the cheapest option#Weighted scores may include cost, but they can include other criteria too.
~C. It ignores categories with low scores#Every category should count according to its weight.
~D. It replaces the need to explain a decision#A weighted score supports a decision, but explanation is still needed.
}
